*.sh text eol=lf
scripts/hooks/* text eol=lf

# Apache vhosts are deployed to a Linux box and byte-compared there by CD.
# CRLF would make every deploy see a spurious change and reload Apache needlessly.
deploy/apache/*.conf text eol=lf
deploy/apache/*.example text eol=lf

# Staging compose is byte-compared on a Linux box by CD; CRLF would fake a change.
deploy/staging/*.yml text eol=lf

# core#1076. Same reason as deploy/apache/ above, one directory over: the files
# install-server-scripts.sh puts on the box, and the hand-applied networkd unit, are
# read on Linux. core#745 removed the second copies of the vhosts; do not bring them
# back under deploy/server/. The duplication guard compares content, and a CRLF
# checkout must not look like a content change.
#
# `deploy/server/*.sh` was already covered by the `*.sh` rule at the top, which is why
# only the .conf/.yml half drifted.
deploy/server/*.conf text eol=lf
deploy/server/*.yml text eol=lf

# The armoured backup public key is one of the five files install-server-scripts.sh puts
# on the box. GnuPG's armour format is line-oriented, so CRLF is a correctness question
# here and not a diff-noise one. Left out of the rule above because it is not a .conf;
# found by test_the_deployed_conf_files_are_pinned_to_lf rather than by anyone noticing.
deploy/server/*.asc text eol=lf

# core#1477. Installed to /etc/cron.d/datanika, where CRLF is not diff noise: cron would
# carry the trailing \r into the command and try to execute `grafana-watchdog.sh\r`, which
# does not exist. The job would be scheduled, present and permanently failing — the same
# "installed, verified, and never runs" shape the cron-in-git change exists to end. Same
# reason as the .asc rule above, and found the same way.
deploy/server/*.cron text eol=lf

# Read by FreeTDS's C parser inside a Linux image; keep it byte-identical across checkouts.
datanika/pymssql_freetds.conf text eol=lf
